<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Project 1 Overview</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00339</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Project 1 Overview</md:title>
    <md:uuid>e86bc070-93be-40a7-b593-bf138bea1bca</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><div class="os-raise-familysupport">
  <para><link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/9cd30c449a9c4122457ca5677b093d433d1351b4">Access the activity sheet</link> and provide copies to your students so they can complete the project.</para>
</div>


<para>In this culminating project, students integrate ideas from the unit. In the first activity, students analyze points on a graph to identify their meaning in context. In the second activity, students identify slopes and intercepts from graphs and match equations to graphs. Finally, students create their own scenarios to match a linear equation.</para>

<title>Project Activities</title>
<list type="enumerated">
  <item>Notice and Wonder: Sugar and Flour <emphasis>(5 minutes)</emphasis></item>
  <item>Matching Matching <emphasis>(30 minutes)</emphasis></item>
</list>

<title>Required Preparation</title>
<list type="enumerated">
  <item><link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b0cae44b0c657ce1b4d861ee8d6f611f530b1372">Print and prepare graph and equation cards</link>, before starting the second activity.</item>
  <item><link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/9cd30c449a9c4122457ca5677b093d433d1351b4">Provide copies of the activity sheet</link> for students to use to collect their thinking.</item>
</list>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <caption>Learning Objectives</caption>
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>Learning Goals (Teacher Facing)</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Learning Targets (Student Facing)</emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
<list>
<item>Analyze the relationship among linear equations, graphs, and verbal descriptions. </item>
</list>
      </entry>
      <entry>
<list>
<item> Identify slopes and intercepts from a graph. </item>
<item>Identify an equation that matches a graph. </item>
<item>Create a real-world scenario from a graph. </item>
</list>
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>



<title>Texas Essential Knowledge and Skills (TEKS)</title>
<list>

  <item>A1(A) apply mathematics to problems arising in everyday life, society, and the workplace</item>
  <item>A1(B) use a problem-solving model that incorporates analyzing given information, formulating a plan or strategy, determining a solution, justifying the solution, and evaluating the problem-solving process and the reasonableness of the solution</item>
  <item>A1(C) select tools, including real objects, manipulatives, paper and pencil, and technology as appropriate, and techniques, including mental math, estimation, and number sense as appropriate, to solve problems</item>
  <item>A1(D) communicate mathematical ideas, reasoning, and their implications using multiple representations, including symbols, diagrams, graphs, and language as appropriate</item>
  <item>A1(E) create and use representations to organize, record, and communicate mathematical ideas</item>
  <item>A1(F) analyze mathematical relationships to connect and communicate mathematical ideas</item>
  <item>A1(G) display, explain, and justify mathematical ideas and arguments using precise mathematical language in written or oral communication.  </item>
  <item>A2(C) write linear equations in two variables given a table of values, a graph, and a verbal description</item>
  <item>A3(A) determine the slope of a line given a table of values, a graph, two points on the line, and an equation written in various forms, including \(y = mx + b\), \(Ax + By = C\), and \(y - y1 = m(x - x1)\) </item>
  <item>A3(C) graph linear functions on the coordinate plane and identify key features, including \(x\)-intercepts, \(y\)-intercepts, zeros, and slope, in mathematical and real-world problems</item>
</list>


</section>
